## Proof

**Strategy for Line:** At each step $n$, Line draws the line $\ell_n$ through $x_n$ perpendicular to the segment $Ox_n$ (from the origin $O$ to $x_n$). If $x_n = O$, Line draws any line through $O$.

**Key geometric fact:** The line through $x_n$ perpendicular to $Ox_n$ is the set of points $y$ satisfying $y \cdot x_n = |x_n|^2$. By Cauchy–Schwarz, every point $y$ on this line satisfies $|y| \geq |y \cdot x_n|/|x_n| = |x_n|$, with equality if and only if $y = x_n$.

**Convergence when Point plays on the boundary:**

Suppose Point always chooses points on the boundary, so $|x_n| = 1$ for all relevant $n$.

When $|x_n| = 1$, the line through $x_n$ perpendicular to $Ox_n$ is the **tangent line** to the unit circle at $x_n$. By the Cauchy–Schwarz argument above, every point $y$ on this tangent line satisfies $|y| \geq 1$, with equality only at $y = x_n$. Therefore, the tangent line intersects the closed unit disc $\overline{D} = \{y : |y| \le 1\}$ **only** at the single point $x_n$.

Since Point must choose $x_{n+1}$ on $\ell_n \cap \overline{D}$, the only available choice is $x_{n+1} = x_n$.

Thus the sequence becomes constant: $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = \cdots$ (if $x_1$ is on the boundary) or becomes constant from $x_2$ onward (if $x_1$ is interior and Point first reaches the boundary at $x_2$). In either case, the sequence converges.

**Maximum distance of the limit:**

Since Point always chooses boundary points, every $x_n$ lies on the unit circle $S^1 = \{y : |y| = 1\}$, which is a **closed** set. Any convergent sequence contained in a closed set has its limit in that set. Therefore the limit point $x$ satisfies $|x| = 1$.

This conclusion is **strategy-independent**: no matter what strategy Line employs, if Point plays on the boundary and the sequence converges, the limit must lie on the unit circle at distance exactly 1 from the origin. Line cannot bring the limit closer to the origin, nor can Point push it farther—all boundary points are equidistant from the origin.

**Conclusion:** The maximum possible distance from the origin to the limit point $x$ is $\boxed{1}$.

